// Seeds/updates the Claude Notes (Sessions 1–14 summaries, each ≤1,000
// words) and the Characters list (each entry ≤100 words) from the local
// session recordings. Claude-authored; the player can edit these. The
// UPDATE only fires while Claude still owns the row (updated_by='claude'),
// so it never overwrites the player's own edits.

const claudeNotes = {"sessions":[{"id":"s1","title":"Session 1 — The High Council","html":"<p><strong>Session 1 — \"The High Council\"</strong> · 10th of Primar, 1224 SE · Highrock, capital of Lorenthar</p>\n\n<p>The session opens in Castle Highrock, seat of Eternium's throne and the Brolin line. <strong>Galeth Holgar</strong> has just returned from the Next Hollow, where — aided by <strong>Lady Elowen</strong>'s aviary — he recovered a runic, foot-cubed metal box from raiders loyal to <strong>Kazar Askelen, the Black Rider</strong>. Its top holds a bowl meant to receive some substance; his experiments do nothing. Over Sorrow's Gulf, an immense first-of-its-kind steel steamship emerges from the fog — a monster built by three houses, capable of carrying an army. In the visitors' wing, shipping magnates <strong>Cato Tulken</strong> and <strong>Jeffrey Vangmore</strong> of Golden Basin Goods watch the same ship and guard a mysterious golden basin.</p>\n\n<h3>Manfred's day</h3>\n<p>Down in the Tidewash, <strong>Manfred Highrock</strong> is summoned from the Saltmeat tavern by his lifelong friend <strong>Rhaegar Blackblade</strong>. En route, a fruit vendor threatens to maim a starving boy with a feather-shaped <em>Sankari</em> blade (which must draw blood when drawn); the High Falconer <strong>Attican</strong> dives from his falcon Shira to stop it. Manfred warns the vendor with the blade's point and claims a Sankari for himself.</p>\n\n<h3>The Council and the charge</h3>\n<p>In the throne room, <strong>King Trask Brolin</strong> — scarred, House Brolin's bear on the wall — names his party the <strong>High Council</strong> and elevates each: Gaius Kratik to Lord Protectorate; Cato and Jeffrey to Lords of Negotiation; <strong>Manfred to Dark Knight of Lorenthar</strong>, a post two centuries empty (the King unsettles him by using his true name, Endrun Ukinri); Attican to guard the road; and the Blood Knights to shield the prince. The King reveals the Fire Duke Branthorp of Esmerath has died, and charges the council to escort <strong>Prince Tobin</strong> — who has never left the city — to witness <strong>Lady Palara</strong>'s ascension in Esmerath, departing in disguise under the Golden Basin banner. He confides he lost his wife to assassins sixteen years ago.</p>\n\n<h3>Omens and secrets</h3>\n<p>A scream interrupts: <strong>Phaedra</strong>, the King's secret Blood Witch, weeps blood and prophesies doom — \"Fire Lake shall burn… the prince—\" before she's dragged away. Afterward, Galeth and Caradoc visit her, and Galeth admits he met a deer-skulled, ram-horned figure during his bloodletting decades ago in a \"land between lives.\" Manfred visits Rhaegar to learn of his new title; Rhaegar — the <strong>Blade of the King</strong>, feared for his unnatural senses — explains the reinstated Dark Knights answer to him, and that Manfred must act where he cannot. That night, High Steward Fraedon tells Gaius who Manfred truly is: an orphan of the Tidewash, raised into the Brotherhood of Swords, risen to <strong>Prince of Swords</strong> — heir to its leadership — before walking away two years ago in search of purpose. Attican flies a lonely night patrol, joined by the silent spirit of his late uncle, the former High Falconer.</p>\n\n<p>Open threads: the runic box and the stolen \"black ledger\"; Kazar Askelen's dark designs; the twin steamships; Phaedra's prophecy; the deer-headed figure who knows Galeth; and the road to Esmerath ahead. (Attican's home, Falcon's Rest, was destroyed months earlier by Kazar — a former Phoenix rider turned terrorist; Attican is its last Falconer.)</p>"},{"id":"s2","title":"Session 2 — The Road to Lake's Hearth","html":"<p><strong>Session 2 — \"The Road to Lake's Hearth\"</strong> · The journey to Esmerath</p>\n\n<p>Disguised as a Golden Basin Goods freight convoy, the High Council travels from Highrock toward <strong>Lake's Hearth</strong>, capital of Esmerath, to witness Lady Palara Branthorp's ascension. Along the way, Gaius mentors the sheltered <strong>Prince Tobin</strong> on war and leadership, and Caradoc arranges a training regimen to toughen the boy up: Galeth for strength, Gaius and Rhaegar for the sword, and Manfred for hand-to-hand.</p>\n\n<h3>Warnings from Veronica</h3>\n<p>A letter from <strong>Veronica Berigog</strong> catalogs the nobles converging on Lake's Hearth and the tensions they carry: the bigoted Baron Fairshell and his daughter (southern Garandale); the Khandamos ruler Lord Urigar Owen and Lady Umara; General Galban Ironhorn (envoy of the Prince of Osmere); one or both Valthar princes; the Jaklu king Alba Velnaia (Jaklu still resents the Valthar for burning the Great Northern Forest ~700 years ago, and Khandamos sided with Valthar then); and King Edward Spikeshield of Thirelia, a friend of the late Duke Branthorp. An ascension, the party notes, is prime ground for subterfuge.</p>\n\n<h3>Falcon's Rest and the tunnels</h3>\n<p>Scouting ahead, Attican spots owl-riders at Falcon's Rest and, with Rhaegar, investigates. They find <strong>Lady Kubrardi Elowen</strong> (ruler of the Next Hollow), her son <strong>Prince Colin</strong>, and <strong>Hunlu Black Talon</strong> (commander of the aviary's owl armada) paying respects at the ruined city's graveyard — Hunlu was a friend of Attican's late father and uncle. Then Shira spots a <strong>Drosvene bat-rider</strong> — Drosvene being an exiled, embargoed pariah state — hiding in the caves. It flees into an enormous circular tunnel, roughly 60–80 feet across, bored clean through the mountain and not there a year ago. Attican and Rhaegar dive after it but lose the trail in the pitch dark.</p>\n\n<p>Back at the convoy, the discovery unsettles everyone. Caradoc connects it to reports of \"sinkholes\" opening across the realm — including one in Selkina, on Esmerath's northeastern edge — and suspects they are deliberately dug tunnels, not natural collapses. He tasks Rhaegar with descending into them (via Drosvene) to learn how deep and how connected they are — but only after the ascension. Attican resolves to go with him.</p>\n\n<h3>A repaired blade</h3>\n<p>As they near the coast, Galeth presents Attican with his late father's dagger, its burned handle replaced by one Galeth hand-carved — a falcon with wings spread. A quiet, emotional moment between men who are brothers in all but blood.</p>\n\n<h3>Lake's Hearth</h3>\n<p>The party reaches Lake's Hearth — a gleaming old-world city of white beaches and finely cut stone on the shore of Fire Lake. Rhaegar peels off to \"meet someone,\" and Manfred, asked to keep an eye on him, tails him to the beach. There Rhaegar meets a beautiful, cloaked woman with wavy hair and sharp blue eyes; they embrace and kiss. Reading Rhaegar's face afterward, Manfred sees something he's never seen on his friend before — frustration laced with fear. A secret Rhaegar guards closely.</p>\n\n<p>Working a dockside tavern, Manfred learns the people adore Lady Palara — a genuine philanthropist who has all but ended poverty for Lake's Hearth's working class. But at the docks he freezes: standing amid arriving Phyrellian nobility is <strong>Aaron</strong>, his old friend from the Brotherhood of Swords, staring at him in surprise — then gone. Rattled, Manfred heads for the palace, quietly casing its security and weighing whether to warn the event's steward about Aaron, or to confide in Rhaegar or Caradoc.</p>\n\n<h3>The Branthorps</h3>\n<p>The rest of the council reaches the unwalled Esmerath Palace, set in sprawling gardens. After Tobin fumbles and then formally announces himself, they're received by Palara's younger siblings — <strong>Prince Harold Branthorp</strong> and <strong>Princess Valenza Branthorp</strong> (a devotee of Golden Basin coffee). Then <strong>Lady Palara Branthorp</strong> herself arrives: poised, blonde, in green silk. She thanks Tobin for coming, houses the council in the Western Tower's top floor, and slips away to preview her royal portrait with Veronica — inviting Cato and Jeffrey along. The session closes on the eve of the ascension.</p>"},{"id":"s3","title":"Session 3 — The Ascension","html":"<p><strong>Session 3 — \"The Ascension\"</strong> · Poison at the feast of Fire Lake</p>\n\n<p>Cato and Jeffrey finally meet <strong>Veronica Berigog</strong> in person — a strikingly beautiful painter with red hair and ice-blue eyes, and the Branthorps' oldest friend. She unveils Lady Palara's royal portrait and briefs them on the tangled tensions among the visiting kingdoms: Valthar and Khandamos against Jaklu; Garandell against Selkinaar and Osmere. An ascension, all agree, is a perfect stage for treachery.</p>\n\n<h3>Gathering guests</h3>\n<p>Manfred, embedded as serving staff with Caradoc's blessing, cases the open-air palace and warns Caradoc that another assassin may be in the city (he'd seen Aaron). The morning brings a crowd of nobles: <strong>Prince Castellon Ardaxes</strong>, one of Velfarion's clever twin phoenix heirs, with four Donflame riders; the Khandamos rulers <strong>Lord Urigar Owen</strong> and <strong>Lady Umara</strong>; the Jaklu king <strong>Alba Velmaier</strong>; General Galban Ironhorn of Osmere; the jovial, hedgehog-blooded <strong>King Edward Spikeshield</strong> of Thirelia; the bigoted <strong>Baron Fairshell</strong> of Garandell and his daughter (whom Manfred recognizes from his past); freight mogul <strong>Ike Sagarmund</strong>; and a suspicious guest, <strong>Andalon Permon</strong>, wearing the pin of a shipping company defunct for a decade. Lady Palara is duly crowned <strong>Lady of Fire Lake</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>The feast — schemes and a murder</h3>\n<p>Over the celebration feast, several threads knot together. Ike Sagarmund quietly asks Cato and Jeffrey to help him hire a Blood Knight to expose his own board's dealings in blood-magic artifacts, so he can seize true control of High Tide Freight. Manfred overhears King Velmaier and Andalon Permon whispering about \"the cover of night.\" And King Edward, drunk, confronts Castellon over shoddy \"Velthar steel.\" In a private moderation led by Palara, Tobin, and Gaius, Castellon reveals a devastating secret: the volcanic forges beneath the Black Mountains are going cold — Velfarion can no longer make its famed steel, and worse, without the volcanoes' heat the Velthar homeland will freeze and empty. Cato and Jeffrey begin scheming to solve it in exchange for influence over Velfarion's succession.</p>\n\n<p>Mid-feast, two aged bear-riders arrive claiming to be sent for Caradoc by \"his oldest friend.\" Galeth fetches him, and the two ride to the docks. Below the deck of a small Lorenthar-flagged boat they find <strong>Erkel Orenfight</strong> — the first Blood Knight of Iosandros, some 700 years old — murdered in his sleep, throat cut. Enraged, Caradoc and Galeth drag a hidden assassin out from a false floor; the killer wears <strong>Brotherhood of Swords</strong> armor, and Caradoc knocks him cold.</p>\n\n<h3>The poisoning</h3>\n<p>Back at the hall, a rich red wine is served as a nightcap — laced with <strong>Lyocane powder</strong> (the very poison Manfred is immune to). Rhaegar's senses catch it a heartbeat too late; he signals the high table to set down their cups, and Cato shouts a warning, but for much of the room it's already done. Guests collapse, bleeding from every orifice: the Jaklu king Alba Velmaier, Baron Fairshell, Lord Urigar Owen and Lady Umara of Khandamos, a Donflame rider, the art dealer Lisa Dermel, and — most cruelly — <strong>Princess Valenza Branthorp</strong>, Palara's young sister and the girl Tobin had been smitten with. A coordinated massacre of northern and southern leaders in a single stroke.</p>\n\n<p>Manfred bolts after the poisoner — Andalon Permon — who sprints to the same balcony where the portrait was shown and leaps 40 feet onto a waiting giant black Phoenix. Manfred lands the campaign's first strike, burying a caustic kunai into the bird with a telekinetic follow-through. The Phoenix answers with a stream of green, napalm-like fire that singes his clothes. As it climbs away southwest over Fire Lake, Manfred sees the rider's true face: <strong>Kazar Askelen, the Black Rider</strong> himself — the terrorist who destroyed Falcon's Rest — had walked among them in disguise and just decapitated half the realm's visiting leadership.</p>\n\n<p>The session ends amid the carnage, with the killer escaped, a first Blood Knight dead, a captured Brotherhood assassin, and the ascension turned to slaughter.</p>"},{"id":"s4","title":"Session 4 — The Aftermath","html":"<p><strong>Session 4 — \"The Aftermath\"</strong> · Interrogations, revelations, and a choice</p>\n\n<p>The poison claimed a devastating toll: the Khandamos queen Lady Umara, Baron Fairshell, the Jaklu king Alba Velmaier, the aviary commander Hunlu Black Talon, the art dealer Lisa Dermel, a Donflame rider, an eagle-rider, and — most painfully — Princess Valenza Branthorp, seventeen, the girl Tobin fancied.</p>\n\n<h3>On the boat</h3>\n<p>Galeth and Caradoc question the captured Brotherhood assassin — revealed to be <strong>Aaron</strong>, Manfred's oldest friend. Caradoc explains that Erkel Orenfight faked his own death after losing his arm; only a handful ever knew he lived — until six months ago, when the King began \"sniffing through Caradoc's ledgers.\" The killing order, Aaron says, came from <strong>Master Ukinri</strong>, head of the Brotherhood of Swords on Sanzori — and any such contract the King would have known of. Suspicion now points squarely at their own king. Caradoc gives Erkel a burning-boat funeral.</p>\n\n<h3>The interrogation</h3>\n<p>Cato and Jeffrey corner the wine mogul <strong>Verna Insholo</strong>, of one of the three insectine families that rule the Pendant Coast. Smug and cryptic, she admits she smuggled in her cousin <strong>Kazar Askelen</strong> and the poison to protect her family from \"most certain death,\" bound by a blood oath of silence, warning that \"what is to come is worse.\" Gaius quietly has the servants scrub her and her guest from the guest list and forget the night.</p>\n\n<p>Two reveals reshape everything. First, Prince Castellon explains Kazar's origin: a former Donflame whose home, the Black Spires, was destroyed decades ago by frozen-north raiders while his king shrugged — grief that curdled into madness. Second, Caradoc realizes where the stolen vault documents went: <strong>Kazar Askelen has made himself a Blood Knight</strong>, using a feather of his rare black Phoenix to work the ritual. That is why he is so terribly powerful — and why Verna warns that the men sent after him will die.</p>\n\n<p>When Verna reveals her oath is unbreakable, Manfred ends her with a touch of death — unsettling the council, who wanted more from her. Rhaegar sends his wolf to find the missing Attican.</p>\n\n<h3>Aaron and the question of loyalty</h3>\n<p>Manfred and Aaron trade barbed words. Aaron warns that the Brotherhood rotted after Manfred left: \"most of the elders are dead — they thought they could stand up to Ukinri,\" and \"you have no idea how bad things got.\" Galeth wants Aaron executed for killing Erkel; Manfred refuses to permit it. Caradoc presses Manfred to choose once and for all — <strong>Dark Knight or Brotherhood assassin</strong> — because the council cannot trust divided loyalties. Manfred, who barely knows these people, bristles at being made to forsake everything he's known.</p>\n\n<h3>The plan</h3>\n<p>With leads pointing to the Brotherhood, the council resolves to sail down Fire Lake and the river to <strong>Sanzori Island</strong> (about five days, via the Next Hollow) to confront Master Ukinri — Manfred guiding them, and Aaron brought along under Tobin's sworn guarantee that he'll reach the island alive. They will not return to Highrock yet; confronting the King directly would only show their hand.</p>\n\n<h3>Rhaegar's confession</h3>\n<p>In private, Rhaegar finally tells Caradoc and Galeth the truth behind his secrets. Eighteen months ago, Khandamos offered the hound-prince's hand to then-Princess Palara to bind their bloodlines; at Palara's request, Rhaegar rode to refuse it — the real reason being that he and <strong>Palara are in love</strong>, forbidden as that is for a Blood Knight. The slighted Lord Urigar sent nearly ten thousand riders after him; Rhaegar killed them all at the dead forest of the southern border — the very slaughter Phaedra foresaw. And then the final blow: <strong>Palara is pregnant</strong> with his child. The session ends on that revelation, the council's secrets — and dangers — multiplying.</p>"},{"id":"s5","title":"Session 5 — United","html":"<p><strong>Session 5 — \"United\"</strong> · The council closes ranks and chooses its road</p>\n\n<p>In the massacre's wake, the Blood Knights are frayed — Galeth reeling from both his brothers' secrets. Rhaegar's wolf Canra and the injured falcon Shira drag the wounded <strong>Attican</strong> back to the hall; Cato, Galeth, and Manfred tend his head wound. When he wakes, Attican reports what knocked him from the sky: <strong>tendrils erupting from the ground</strong> near the aviaries, reaching for the owls — yet another buried menace. He also recounts a stranger vision from that morning: an otherworldly man named <strong>Quigley Venturi</strong>, bearing a strange iron weapon and vanishing without trace, who spoke a prophecy — \"hope lies within the Black Mountains,\" \"there are kings that walk among you,\" \"if you seek truth, go to the Redwood\" — and claimed to have known the first Falcon Rider generations ago. Rhaegar and Caradoc, unsettled, admit they have met this figure too.</p>\n\n<h3>Brothers, revealed</h3>\n<p>Apart from the others, Manfred and <strong>Aaron</strong> finally speak as the brothers they are. Aaron lays the Brotherhood bare: Master <strong>Ukinri</strong> took them off the streets as children precisely because children are easy to shape, and the \"good of the realm\" they were raised on was a leash. After Manfred walked away, members left in waves. A year ago the elders confronted Ukinri with an old clause — that an unwilling heir holds no claim above a loyal elder — and he killed them all but one, then burned the Hall of Histories to rewrite it in his image. Fewer than twenty remain. Ukinri had lied to Aaron about Erkel's identity to secure the contract; Aaron learned the truth only when captured. Manfred offers him a new path: to help rebuild the <strong>Dark Knights</strong> together, not as Ukinri's dogs but as something better. Aaron accepts.</p>\n\n<h3>The council becomes one</h3>\n<p>Manfred formally sets the Brotherhood behind him and commits to the council; Aaron is allowed to travel with them, unarmed and watched, under Tobin's guarantee. Rhaegar clarifies Manfred's role at last: the Dark Knight answers to no one, an autonomous instrument meant to act where Rhaegar cannot — and free to recruit ranks of his own. Privately, Caradoc warns Galeth of a grimmer thing: no woman has ever survived carrying a Blood Knight's child, and <strong>Palara may not survive her pregnancy</strong>. Cato rallies the group; for the first time the whole party — Blood Knights, Dark Knight, Falconer, Lords, and prince — stands united and, mostly, trusting.</p>\n\n<h3>Where to next</h3>\n<p>Weighing their tangled leads — Sanzori and Ukinri, the bat-riders and tunnels, the Insholo conspiracy, and the failing Velthar steel — the council settles on <strong>World's Peak</strong>, the ancient capital of Velfarion in the Black Mountains. Prince Castellon (an old childhood friend of Attican's) extends an invitation framed as cultural education for Tobin, but the real aims are plain: investigate why the volcanic forges are dying, secure the steel the realm depends on before winter, perhaps find the tunnels' connection, and quietly gain sway over Velfarion's succession. They will bring Tobin. Threaded through it all: talk of prophecy, of \"kings who walk among us,\" and of the strange sword Manfred carries — the sword of the undying king. To the Black Mountains they go.</p>"},{"id":"s6","title":"Session 6 — The Iron Road","html":"<p><strong>Session 6 — \"The Iron Road\"</strong> · Dreams, dead-world relics, and the road north</p>\n\n<p>The morning after the massacre brings a rash of visions. Manfred, through his dreamwalking, sees war being readied in a jade-domed city of Garandell — pike-laden wagons, an armored war-crab, and a camp of thousands on the hills. It fits a darker suspicion: Baron Fairshell's grieving daughter may goad Garandell into blaming Selkinaar for his death.</p>\n\n<h3>Mr. Bones</h3>\n<p>Galeth, Rhaegar, and Caradoc compare the figure some of them met in their bloodletting — a robed skeleton with a deer's skull and a ram's horns, standing before a floating mountain (the party takes to calling him \"Mr. Bones\"). He told Galeth he could \"save everyone or destroy everything,\" and asked Rhaegar \"out of a trillion souls, what makes you special?\" Caradoc saw no such thing. Then Jeffrey wakes from a dream of the same entity beside a green-lit <strong>Black Altar</strong> — a portal opening, bats, Phoenixes, and Shira wreathed in lightning — and, atop the altar, a cube and a pyramid. Manfred admits he dreamed of Mr. Bones as a child, the very morning Ukinri found him. Four of the nine have now met this thing; it is stirring.</p>\n\n<h3>The triad of relics</h3>\n<p>The dreams connect three dead-world artifacts. The <strong>Black Altar</strong>, a madness-inducing relic, was one Cato and Jeffrey were hired by the Pendant Coast council to recover in their past — they found it ringed by a hundred suicides, watched it break Captain Kellogg's crew, lied that it was already gone, and sank it in the Broken Spear Shallows. Caradoc reveals he first quarantined that altar on its far eastern island centuries ago (\"I went with 35 men and came back with 3\"). The second relic is the runic <strong>cube</strong> — Galeth's blood-box — still locked in his lab beneath Highrock. The third, a <strong>pyramid</strong>, is unaccounted for. Fearing Mr. Bones wants them united, the council resolves to keep the three apart.</p>\n\n<h3>War in the south</h3>\n<p>Gaius receives a warning from his old friend <strong>Perrin</strong>, a war-engineer for King Parentstep of Garandell: the Garandell army is in full mobilization, marching somewhere — likely Selkinaar or the disputed Osmere border. Gaius quietly alerts Selkinaar under a Blood Knight's name and warns Osmere's General Galban Ironhorn, who reacts with unsettling eagerness — as if he'd welcome the war.</p>\n\n<h3>Departure</h3>\n<p>King Edward Spikeshield relays that Castellon has gone ahead to bury his dead and awaits them at <strong>World's Peak</strong>, the ancient capital of Velfarion. The plan sets: sail across Fire Lake on Edward's iron ship to Thirelia, then take the <strong>Iron Road</strong> — the mountain tunnel-road Galeth himself once led the brigade to build — up to World's Peak, with Tobin coming along under the guise of education. Lady Palara sees the council off warmly, thanking them for the many they saved; Manfred notices Rhaegar now wears a silver swan pendant, a token of her. Aboard, Aaron warns Manfred that Velfarion is home to <strong>Steiger</strong>, another former Brotherhood member who left bitter and blames Manfred for the order's collapse — a reunion unlikely to be friendly. As the ship pulls from shore, Manfred begins Tobin's training, Karate-Kid style, and the council sails toward the Black Mountains and a lake that, at sunrise, will show why it is called Fire.</p>"},{"id":"s7","title":"Session 7 — The Siren's Song","html":"<p><strong>Session 7 — \"The Siren's Song\"</strong> · Across Fire Lake to a stolen throne</p>\n\n<p>Sailing on, Manfred continues Tobin's training, coaxing the sheltered prince — who has never truly made a choice for himself — to begin deciding for himself. Meanwhile Cato and Jeffrey strike a deal with King Edward: as Thirelia localizes its own steel production and pulls back the iron it once shipped north, Golden Basin Goods will quietly supplement Velfarion's iron supply, keeping the north stocked and earning the council goodwill.</p>\n\n<h3>The siren attack</h3>\n<p>A siren song — which snares only human men — rolls across the water. Rhaegar catches it first and shouts for wax; Manfred lashes himself to the mast. But the entranced, sleepwalking <strong>Tobin</strong> climbs the rail and dives overboard. Rhaegar plunges after him, Galeth close behind, into a pod of six shark-tailed sirens. They haul the prince out and beat the creatures back — Galeth's rhino-tough skin turning teeth, Rhaegar's fire scattering the rest. To snap Tobin free of the song, Manfred works a rune spell — a protective field — openly revealing that he, too, wields <strong>rune (blood) magic</strong> akin to the Blood Knights'. Galeth, the realm's foremost authority on such magic, is fascinated, and the two begin comparing craft; Manfred learns he can conjure \"blood needles.\" (Jeffrey, unbothered, slept through the whole ordeal.)</p>\n\n<h3>Hedgehome and Fire Lake</h3>\n<p>At winter sunrise the lake earns its name: light refracting off the ice-capped Black Mountains pours into the water in rippling waves of orange and red. The ship reaches <strong>Hedgehome</strong>, capital of Thirelia — a city of dark mining-stone where colossal iron-backed hedgehogs haul ore through streets built wide to let them pass.</p>\n\n<h3>A coup in the mountains</h3>\n<p>At the dock, Edward brings grim news. In the south, Garandell and Selkinaar are arming for war while Osmere readies a surprise strike — a three-way conflict looms. And in Velfarion, King <strong>Ripavan Ardaxes</strong> has fallen into a coma. With only one prince present, Castellon's twin <strong>Amari</strong> declared himself intermediary ruler — and when Castellon returned and objected, Amari chased him from the palace. Castellon lives, hidden somewhere in <strong>World's Peak</strong>. Edward gives Cato and Jeffrey a letter of recommendation about the iron trade to use as cover to enter the palace and find Castellon, while asking that Thirelia be kept out of Velfarion's civil strife.</p>\n\n<h3>The Iron Road</h3>\n<p>The council rides north to the mouth of the <strong>Iron Road</strong> — a vast tunnel, three hundred feet wide and torch-lit, that Galeth himself once led the brigade to carve (and where he lost the sight in one eye; thousands died in its making). It is a four-day march through the dark — a sensory torment for Rhaegar — to reach World's Peak. Attican and Shira fly ahead to meet them there. Their aim on arrival is plain: find Castellon before dealing with Amari, and tread carefully into a palace where a brother has stolen a throne.</p>"},{"id":"s8","title":"Session 8 — World's Peak","html":"<p><strong>Session 8 — \"World's Peak\"</strong> · A divided city and a king full of worms</p>\n\n<p>Emerging from the Iron Road on the fourth day, the Blood Knights don plain cloaks and wrap their weapons to avoid inflaming a city on edge. On the road they meet <strong>Thorgan Hagorn</strong>, a badger-riding envoy of a minor northern house, sent to decide which twin to back. He shares the crisis in full: two rival speed-hawks have gone out — Castellon's accusing <strong>Amari</strong> of poisoning their father and seizing the throne, and Amari's claiming their comatose father privately named <em>him</em> the elder heir and pinning the poisoning on Castellon. Houses across the north are choosing sides; civil war looms. Rhaegar steers the group toward a quieter district, Feather's End.</p>\n\n<h3>The city in the valley</h3>\n<p>World's Peak sits in a bowl between three volcanoes: the ancient blackstone Palace of Flame, the Donflame's Flaming Roost on one wall, and the crumbling Temple of the Dead Gods — its featureless giant statues now sheltering the city's homeless — on another. At the center stands a faceless statue of <strong>Dahlia of the Black Spires</strong>, King Ripavan's beloved first wife, whose death forty years ago changed him (and whose home, the Black Spires, ties her to Kazar Askelen). Beneath the grandeur, the people live in despair: legal indentured servitude has trapped whole streets in debt-slavery to textile mogul <strong>Gatten Strayger</strong>. Cato aids one destitute family, and the council leaves the encounter quietly furious. In the square, a herald proclaiming \"Lord Regent Amari\" is nearly drowned out by a divided, brawling crowd.</p>\n\n<h3>The Palace of Flame</h3>\n<p>At the palace they meet <strong>Amari Ardaxes</strong> — the warrior twin, muscle-bound and confident, now styling himself Lord Regent — flanked by advisors <strong>Selmani Parwisk</strong> (overseer of the Iron Road) and <strong>Orton Helen Elleby</strong> (close to the king, and somehow off). Amari assumes the council came by owl; they let the lie stand. He welcomes their scrutiny, certain the truth will exonerate him, and grudgingly allows them to see his father — but something in his deflection suggests he is hiding more than grief. On the stairs, Selmani quietly steers them to the truth: <strong>Castellon is in hiding in the Temple of the Dead Gods</strong> — go there, and say Selmani sent you.</p>\n\n<h3>The thing in the king</h3>\n<p>Examining the comatose <strong>King Ripavan</strong>, Cato and Manfred find no trace of poison at all. Instead, running a hand over his arm, Cato feels something move beneath the skin — and uncovers <strong>parasites</strong>: four-inch, tendril-legged creatures burrowing through the king's flesh, five or six of them across his chest, non-magical yet utterly unknown to any of them. This is no ordinary illness, and the king is plainly not being cared for as he should be. Unwilling to experiment on another realm's monarch, the council sketches the creature and resolves to consult Rhaegar — who knows the city's underbelly — and the Flaming Roost's flockmaster, <strong>Vuvajek Biddlebug</strong>. They slip out of the palace toward Feather's End and the hidden Castellon, the mystery of the parasites — and their likely tie to the tunnels and tendrils seen elsewhere — hanging over them.</p>"},{"id":"s9","title":"Session 9 — The Heart of the Mountain","html":"<p><strong>Session 9 — \"The Heart of the Mountain\"</strong> · Bloodworms, a stolen relic, and a black lake of memory</p>\n\n<p>Council member <strong>Selmani Parwisk</strong> — an old, discreet connection of Cato and Jeffrey, who wears a mind-reading amulet they once sold him — becomes the party's guide to World's Peak's rot. He lays out the players: the cold advisor <strong>Helen Elleby</strong>, architect (with King Ripavan) of the debt-slavery that grips the city's poor, now steering Amari toward abolishing wage floors entirely — true slavery. And Castellon, brilliant and ruthless, who has always meant to seize the throne and hoard the realm's Velthar steel to build Velfarion's army. Meanwhile Attican reunites with the flockmaster <strong>Vuvajek Biddlebug</strong>, an old family friend and councilman, who covers the party's \"owl\" lie and warns him to guard his friendship with the warring twins.</p>\n\n<h3>The relic and the worms</h3>\n<p>Selmani reveals the crisis beneath the crisis. The <strong>Heart of the Mountain</strong> is not a metaphor but a literal relic — a small <strong>pyramid</strong>-shaped dead-world artifact that has kept the three volcanoes burning for millennia. It was stolen some three weeks ago from its pedestal above the magma, and only House Ardaxes and the six-member Velfar Council knew it existed — a tiny suspect pool. It is, unmistakably, the third piece of the artifact triad the party has been dreading (Black Altar, cube, and now this pyramid).</p>\n\n<p>And the king's illness is no illness. Shown the party's sketch, Rhaegar names the creatures under Ripavan's skin: <strong>bloodworms</strong> — mutant parasites bred in pools of blood, swallowed as pebble-sized eggs and impossible to remove. The king was deliberately fed his death, likely through his food. He is beyond saving. Suspicion tightens around Castellon — the theft, the framing of a poisoning, the convenient summons of the High Council all read as his scheme rather than the warrior Amari's — though nothing is certain.</p>\n\n<h3>The black lake</h3>\n<p>Rhaegar, drawn off by a woman going blind into second sight who kept whispering his name, returns with a lead of his own. She spoke of the <strong>Black Lake</strong> in the Dead Forest of Selkinaar — a place said to show the memories of anyone whose blood you carry into it. She saw Rhaegar there, beneath its surface, learning at last who his parents were. Beyond his personal longing, it offers a tool: with a drop of the dying king's blood, the lake could reveal which twin was truly born first and settle the succession Ripavan never would. Rhaegar quietly hands Manfred a vial and asks him to steal a blood sample from the king that night.</p>\n\n<h3>To the Temple of the Dead Gods</h3>\n<p>As dusk falls, the council climbs the steps of the Temple of the Dead Gods, where <strong>Castellon</strong> hides among his supporters and the city's homeless. They settle on a plan: let Attican, his childhood friend, draw him out and bait him about the Heart of the Mountain — if Castellon shares the family secret, he genuinely wants their help; if he hides it, he is hiding much more. Beneath the maneuvering runs a colder calculus — slavery versus a frozen realm without Velthar steel, and the growing sense that whoever schemed all this may still make the better king. Winter is coming to World's Peak, and the council must choose. The session ends on the temple stairs, on the verge of meeting the hidden prince.</p>"},{"id":"s10","title":"Session 10 — The Prince in the Temple","html":"<p><strong>Session 10 — \"The Prince in the Temple\"</strong> · A confession beneath the dead gods</p>\n\n<p>Cato, Manfred, Gaius, and Rhaegar climb the four crowded tiers of the Temple of the Dead Gods, seeking the hidden Castellon, while Galeth, Caradoc, and Attican watch the stairs. On the way they pass a blind blood-seer, <strong>Lady Elba</strong>, who reads the bones for each of them. Her words to the others are cryptic warnings, but for <strong>Rhaegar</strong> her eyes turn to ink and her voice with them: \"You will bring pain and suffering to everyone you love… I see a trillion souls marching into a fiery pit, and I see you leading them there\" — the same doom Mr. Bones has whispered. Rhaegar, rattled, silences her and moves on; Manfred quietly pockets the blood-blackened bones.</p>\n\n<h3>Castellon</h3>\n<p>On the top level they find <strong>Castellon</strong> among a dozen disguised Donflame. He receives them as the trade envoys who answered his call, and — pressed — lays the secret bare: the Heart of the Mountain is a real <strong>pyramid relic</strong> that sits beneath the northern volcano and keeps the fires alive, and it has been stolen. Only a handful ever knew it existed: the twins, the comatose king, councilman Selmani, the flockmaster Biddlebug, and Amari's backers — advisor <strong>Helen Elleby</strong> (tied to Garandell), the slaver-mogul <strong>Gatten Strayger</strong> (the very Lord Strayger whose debt-slavery grips the poor), and ground-forces commander <strong>Zato Ulrich</strong>. Castellon paints Amari as a short-sighted tool of these plutocrats, poised to abolish wage floors and sell Velfarion's people into slavery.</p>\n\n<p>Then, cornered, Castellon confesses outright: <strong>he stole the Heart of the Mountain</strong>. Not for chaos, but to make his people strong and free. He resents that Lorenthar rules the realm on the strength of a prophecy carved in stone while giving nothing — and lays out his true design: not conquest by fire, but by <em>dependency</em>. Build the kingdoms up on Velthar steel, then slowly choke the supply until all Iosandros kneels to Velfarion. He will not let his people freeze this winter; the Heart is his leverage, and he cannot simply return it while Amari sits the throne to claim the credit. He means to have Amari executed if he wins — and knows he'll die or be enslaved if he loses. He recounts a history lesson to justify himself: how self-reliant Selkinaar endured its ruin while dependent Garandell grew soft, and how Lorenthar helped one and abandoned the other.</p>\n\n<h3>An impossible choice</h3>\n<p>The council leaves with the twins' war laid out as a trap with no clean exit: back the greedy but weaker <strong>Amari</strong>, who keeps Velfarion harmless but damns its people to slavery; or the brilliant, dangerous <strong>Castellon</strong>, who would end the slavery but bends the whole realm toward Velfarion's dominion — and holds the realm's warmth hostage. They float a moderated meeting between the brothers (framed as a diplomacy lesson for Tobin), which Castellon will accept if Amari does. And Rhaegar reminds them of the quieter task: when that meeting comes, Manfred slips away to steal a drop of the dying king's blood for the Black Lake — to learn at last which twin was truly born first, and settle the succession the old king never would. The session ends with the party deadlocked, most leaning toward Castellon, and no third path yet in sight.</p>"},{"id":"s11","title":"Session 11 — The Blood Oath","html":"<p><strong>Session 11 — \"The Blood Oath\"</strong> · A stolen sample, a duel declared, and the secret that undoes a dynasty</p>\n\n<p>Still weighing the twins, the council visits the flockmaster <strong>Vuvajek Biddlebug</strong>, who quietly confesses he uses the Roost's inflated budget to buy indebted families out of debt-slavery and give them honest work — but warns that the people have lost all hope, and no uprising will come. He's troubled to learn Castellon stole the Heart, and insists the Ardaxes line must rule, for it is one of the few things that still unites Velfarion.</p>\n\n<h3>The king's blood</h3>\n<p>While Caradoc and Galeth go to propose the mediated meeting, Manfred slips into the comatose king's chamber to steal a blood sample for the Black Lake. He finds <strong>Selmani Parwisk</strong> keeping a nightly vigil there — an old friend of the king — who lets him draw it. Selmani reveals the cruelest turn of the succession: King Ripavan never told a soul which twin was born first, and the only ones who knew — the maester who delivered them and a bedmaid — were both hanged the day after. The answer died with them. In the room, Manfred finds only a near-empty journal, its one tender entry mourning Dahlia of the Black Spires, the king's lost first love.</p>\n\n<h3>Aaron gone</h3>\n<p>Back at Feathers End, <strong>Aaron</strong> — set to watch over Tobin — has vanished without a word, his drink left full on the railing. Tobin sleeps on, unharmed, but the disappearance sits wrong with everyone.</p>\n\n<h3>The blood oath</h3>\n<p>Then Rhaegar binds Manfred and Cato in a <strong>blood oath</strong> and, unable now to lie, reveals the secret kept by only a handful. Sixteen years ago, blood cultists attacked the royal parade celebrating the prince's birth. The queen was killed — and so, unknown to the realm, was the infant prince. The King was left sterile. The boy who travels with them as <strong>Tobin Brolin is an imposter</strong>, a substitute raised in the dead heir's place — not Trask's son (the king's eyes are blue; Tobin's are brown). Because the King can sire no more children and the true heir is dead, the Brolin dynasty — <strong>Eternium</strong> — ends the moment the king dies. The prophecy is only a hope now; to reveal Tobin as false would drown the realm in chaos, which is why the Blood Knights and Gaius guard the lie. It is said the \"Undying King\" will one day reveal himself. Caradoc is furious that Rhaegar broke faith to tell it; the weight of it, and of Palara's pregnancy and the king's coming death, presses on them all.</p>\n\n<h3>A duel of heirs</h3>\n<p>Caradoc and Galeth return with Amari's answer: he'll meet his brother — but not to talk. Invoking his right, <strong>Amari declares a duel of heirs</strong>, a fight to the death. If Castellon accepts, Amari means to kill him; if he refuses, he forfeits his claim. It is set for three days hence, at sunset under a full moon, in the city square before Dahlia's faceless statue, witnessed by the High Council. Amari, the warrior, is certain of victory over his bookish brother — though Castellon may name a champion.</p>\n\n<p>With the clock running, Rhaegar and Attican depart at once for the <strong>Black Lake</strong> in the Dead Forest, carrying the king's blood, to learn before the duel which twin was truly born first. The council is left holding an impossible secret, a vanished friend, and a duel that will decide who rules the realm's warmth.</p>"},{"id":"s12","title":"Session 12 — The Undying King","html":"<p><strong>Session 12 — \"The Undying King\"</strong> · A duel, a revelation, and a throne divided</p>\n\n<p>In the days before the duel, the council warns Castellon it will be no mere talk — his brother means to fight to the death. Castellon is unbothered: he will accept, front no champion, and at the moment blades are drawn reveal that he stole the Heart of the Mountain, using Amari's shock against him. Meanwhile Manfred trains Tobin, who wanders the Temple of the Dead Gods in a melancholy fog — brooding on why the gods abandoned the world, on his distant father, and on all the death he has seen since leaving his palace walls.</p>\n\n<h3>The duel</h3>\n<p>At dusk beneath the faceless statue of Dahlia, Amari arrives flanked by his slaver-backers <strong>Helen Elleby</strong> and <strong>Gatten Strayger</strong>, and formally challenges Castellon. Castellon chooses the rapier — and, mid-fight, calmly announces he stole the Heart. The revelation rattles Amari, and the bookish brother lands hit after hit, though neither can bring himself to kill the other. Amari finally disarms Castellon and drops him — and then, from the sky, <strong>Rhaegar and Attican</strong> descend into the square.</p>\n\n<h3>Gaedric Ardaxes</h3>\n<p>Rhaegar reveals the truth the Black Lake showed him in the king's own memories: his mother was <strong>Dahlia of the Black Spires</strong>, Ripavan's first and truest love, and his father is <strong>King Ripavan Ardaxes</strong>. Rhaegar Blackblade — the orphaned Blood Knight — is <strong>Gaedric Ardaxes</strong>, the king's firstborn, older than either twin, and the rightful heir to the Black Throne. As he speaks, the three volcanoes rumble as if in answer. But Rhaegar does not want the crown; he has been Blackblade all his life. As the true heir, he holds the right to name a successor — and, refusing to decide which half-brother dies, hands the choice to the <strong>High Council</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>A throne divided</h3>\n<p>Rather than choose one, the council brokers a shared rule: neither brother dies. Under the oversight of the High Council of Lorenthar, <strong>Castellon</strong> will command the Donflame and <strong>Amari</strong> will govern the trade of Velthar steel — a separation of powers, each checking the other. Both, reluctantly, agree. Rhaegar then executes the two architects of Velfarion's slavery, Helen Elleby and Gatten Strayger, on the spot. And Castellon reveals where he stashed the Heart of the Mountain: in the Flaming Roost, hidden with the flockmaster <strong>Vuvajek Biddlebug</strong> — who was in on it all along.</p>\n\n<p>Rhaegar's identity must stay buried forever for the realm's stability. His unborn child with Palara, whose bloodline his abdication disqualifies, will be raised in Velfarion under the guise of a brother's child — sworn to secrecy by blood oath, to carry a claim in the years to come. It is a bittersweet close: Rhaegar, for the first time in his life, knows where he comes from. The sunken shadow lifts from his face. \"I was never meant to be a king,\" he tells his wolf, \"but it is nice to know where I come from. I don't have to wander anymore.\" Bloodlessly, Velfarion's civil war ends — and a new era begins.</p>"},{"id":"s13","title":"Session 13 — The Empty Box","html":"<p><strong>Session 13 — \"The Empty Box\"</strong> · <em>Reconstructed from Session 14's recap — the Session 13 recording wasn't available, so this is a brief account rather than a full summary.</em></p>\n\n<p>The council confronted flockmaster <strong>Vuvajek Biddlebug</strong> at the Flaming Roost about the Heart of the Mountain. He began leading them toward its hiding place — but as they neared it, the floor gave way, and Biddlebug, Manfred, and others dropped into a tunnel swarming with <strong>Insholo ant-riders</strong>. In the fight that followed, Biddlebug was killed and dragged off; Manfred found only his severed leg. Galeth felled one ant-rider with a single blow.</p>\n\n<p>Searching the Roost's great piles of straw, the party found the relic's chest — with a cut-out alcove shaped for the pyramid — <strong>empty</strong>. The Heart of the Mountain was gone, evidently taken by the Insholo ants, who had tunneled with uncanny precision straight to it. That night, Cato overheard Rhaegar and the twins puzzling over how the volcanoes had roared back to life with the Heart still missing; Rhaegar's best guess was that one of the prophecies had been fulfilled. With the relic stolen and its thieves pointing east, the council turned its attention homeward — to secure the cube before anyone came for it too.</p>"},{"id":"s14","title":"Session 14 — The Road Home","html":"<p><strong>Session 14 — \"The Road Home\"</strong> · Homeward to Highrock, and a war already begun</p>\n\n<p>Before dawn, the sleepless Manfred walks World's Peak with Rhaegar. Each carries a weight: Rhaegar the ten thousand riders he killed at the dead forest, Manfred the child he once spared. That child, he confesses, was <strong>Cosca Fairshell</strong> — Baron Fairshell's daughter — whom a Brotherhood contract had marked, and whose hatred is now helping drive Garandell toward war with Selkinaar. Master Ukinri called his mercy weakness; Rhaegar tells him sparing a child was no failing, and that this war was likely never his to stop.</p>\n\n<h3>Loose ends in Velfarion</h3>\n<p>The council settles its next moves: recover the runic cube from Highrock's vault before it too is stolen, then turn east toward the Pendant Coast and the Insholo — the ant-riding conspirators who took the Heart of the Mountain and who seem able to <em>sense</em> dead-world relics. The volcanoes burn again despite the Heart's absence, a mystery Rhaegar lays to fulfilled prophecy. Councilman <strong>Selmani Parwisk</strong> sees them off, pledging to keep the twins in line and to write if anything stirs. Then, on borrowed Phoenixes, the party flies home over the mountains.</p>\n\n<h3>Highrock and the cube</h3>\n<p>They reach Castle Highrock by night. Galeth descends to his lab in Gelfric's Garrison to find it ransacked — but the <strong>cube</strong> still on the counter. As he lifts it, a flash of the dream-realm strikes him: floating mountains, giant trees, the ground of Mr. Bones. He secures it, and later concludes there is no safe way to seal or destroy such a relic — dead-world artifacts are too volatile, prone to catastrophic release. The council plans how to face King Trask in the morning: claim credit for averting Velfarion's civil war and settling its succession (a lesson for Tobin), while burying everything dangerous — Erkel's murder, Rhaegar and Palara, the stolen Heart, Kazar's rise. They remain wary of a king they suspect ordered a Blood Knight's death, and unsure he'll bless taking Tobin toward the fighting.</p>\n\n<h3>War, and a bloodline revealed</h3>\n<p>High Steward <strong>Fraedon Aloware</strong> brings the news: the southern war is no longer looming but real — Garandell and Selkinaar clashed three mornings ago with heavy losses on both sides, and Osmere has yet to arrive. Worse, <strong>Kazar Askelen</strong>, the Black Rider, strikes at <em>both</em> armies, fighting for no one, sowing pure chaos — and he is now a Blood Knight who must be stopped. Then Rhaegar closes one more circle: his mother, Dahlia of the Black Spires, was truly <strong>Dahlia Askelen</strong> — making Rhaegar kin to Kazar himself. With the Black Rider's hand seemingly behind everything, the council resolves to follow him east. They will face the king at dawn, and the campaign turns toward the Pendant Coast, the Insholo, and the war Kazar is feeding.</p>"}],"activeId":"s1"};

const characters = {"html":"<h3>The High Council — the party Manfred travels with</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Rhaegar Blackblade</strong> — Manfred's lifelong friend and now his commander: the Blade of the King and head of the reinstated Dark Knights. A Blood Knight (~50, looks younger). Revealed to be <em>Gaedric Ardaxes</em>, eldest son of Velfarion's King Ripavan and Dahlia of the Black Spires — the true heir, who abdicated to stay Blackblade. Secret lover of Lady Palara, who carries his child. Slew ten thousand Khandamos riders at the dead forest. A seer foretold he will lead \"a trillion souls into a fiery pit.\" Kin, by blood, to Kazar Askelen. Rides the wolf Canra.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Galeth Holgar</strong> — Blood Knight, a giant red-haired warrior and the realm's foremost expert on rune magic; he led the brigade that carved the Iron Road, losing an eye to it. He met the deer-skulled figure (\"Mr. Bones\") in a bloodletting vision, and now keeps the runic cube — one of three dead-world relics — locked away.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Gaius Kratik</strong> — Maester to Tobin, now Lord Protectorate and the boy's guardian; a one-eyed former war-table commander. Steady and wise, he carries the council's darkest secrets, including Tobin's true nature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Attican</strong> — the High Falconer, the realm's last Falconer, who rides the great falcon Shira. A childhood friend of the Ardaxes twins; his home, Falcon's Rest, was destroyed by Kazar Askelen. He flew the king's blood to the Black Lake with Rhaegar.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cato Tulken</strong> — co-founder of Golden Basin Goods and a Lord of Negotiation; a sharp, empathetic dealmaker who brokered the twins' shared rule and helps the realm's poor where he can.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jeffrey Vangmore</strong> — co-founder of Golden Basin Goods and Lord of Negotiation, Cato's partner and logistician. Aromantic (unmoved by the sirens' song), he has prophetic dreams of the Black Altar.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Aaron</strong> — Manfred's oldest friend and fellow Tidewash orphan; a Brotherhood assassin who killed Erkel on a lie, then reconciled with Manfred and joined the council, meaning to help rebuild the Dark Knights. Watches over Tobin.</p>\n\n<h3>The Court of Highrock (Lorenthar)</h3>\n\n<p><strong>King Trask Brolin</strong> — the High King (House Brolin, bear sigil), who named the council and granted Manfred his title. Sterile and embittered since assassins killed his wife sixteen years ago; the party suspects he ordered the first Blood Knight's murder.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Prince \"Tobin Brolin\"</strong> — the 16-year-old heir the council escorts and trains. Secretly an <em>imposter</em>: the true infant prince died in that same assassination, and Tobin is no son of Trask. When the sterile king dies, the Brolin dynasty — Eternium — ends. Sheltered, grieving, and slowly coming into himself.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Caradoc Maxim</strong> — Blood Knight and Brigadier General; Erkel's oldest friend and keeper of the succession secret. Reinstated the Dark Knights under Rhaegar's command.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Fraedon Aloware</strong> — High Steward and the King's advisor; knows Manfred's history and brings the council news of the wider realm.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Phaedra</strong> — the King's secret Blood Witch, kept beneath the castle; a blood seer who weeps blood and foretold Fire Lake's burning and doom for the prince.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Erkel Orenfight</strong> — the first Blood Knight of Iosandros (~700 years old), who faked his death and lived in hiding until Aaron murdered him on a contract — one the King likely sanctioned.</p>\n\n<h3>Velfarion &amp; World's Peak</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Castellon Ardaxes</strong> — the cunning Velfar twin and Attican's old friend, who stole the Heart of the Mountain to force Velfarion's independence. Now co-rules Velfarion, commanding the Donflame, under the High Council's oversight.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Amari Ardaxes</strong> — the warrior twin, who seized the throne and leaned on slaver backers. After the bloodless duel, he co-rules as the public face, governing the Velthar-steel trade.</p>\n\n<p><strong>King Ripavan Ardaxes</strong> — Velfarion's king, comatose from bloodworms fed to him. Rhaegar's true father; he never revealed which heir was elder.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Selmani Parwisk</strong> — Velfar councilman overseeing the Iron Road, an old connection of Cato and Jeffrey and the council's trusted eyes in World's Peak; wears a mind-reading amulet.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Vuvajek Biddlebug</strong> — the flockmaster of the Flaming Roost, an old friend of Attican's family who secretly hid the Heart of the Mountain for Castellon. Killed by Insholo ant-riders.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Helen Elleby &amp; Gatten Strayger</strong> — the cold advisor and the greasy slaver-mogul who built Velfarion's debt-slavery. Both executed by Rhaegar at the duel.</p>\n\n<h3>Esmerath &amp; the wider realm</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Lady Palara Branthorp</strong> — Lady of Fire Lake, a beloved philanthropist. Rhaegar's secret lover, now carrying his child — a pregnancy that may cost her life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Prince Harold Branthorp</strong> — Palara's brother, who lost their sister Valenza to the ascension poisoning.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Veronica Berigog</strong> — a strikingly beautiful Esmerathian painter, the Branthorps' oldest friend, and a valuable informant to the council.</p>\n\n<p><strong>King Edward Spikeshield</strong> — the hearty, jovial king of Thirelia and a friend of the late Fire Duke; ferried the council across Fire Lake and gave them cover to enter Velfarion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ike Sagarmund</strong> — mogul of High Tide Freight, a rival of Golden Basin Goods, who wants a Blood Knight to expose his own board's blood-magic dealings so he can seize control.</p>\n\n<h3>Antagonists &amp; the gathering war</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Kazar Askelen, \"the Black Rider\"</strong> — the campaign's great villain. A former Donflame, driven mad when his home (the Black Spires) was destroyed, now a terrorist who made himself a Blood Knight. He poisoned the Esmerath ascension in disguise and now strikes both armies in the southern war, fighting for no one but chaos. Rides the last black-spire Phoenix — and is kin to Rhaegar through Dahlia Askelen.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Insholo</strong> — an ant-riding family of the Pendant Coast's three-house council, dealing in dead-world relics and able to sense them. They engineered the ascension massacre (through Verna Insholo, Kazar's cousin, whom Manfred killed) and stole the Heart of the Mountain.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cosca Fairshell</strong> — Baron Fairshell's daughter, the child Manfred once spared, now goading Garandell toward war with Selkinaar.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Master Ukinri</strong> — tyrant-master of the Brotherhood of Swords, who murdered its elders and burned its histories, and whose contract sent Aaron to kill Erkel.</p>\n\n<h3>The looming mystery</h3>\n\n<p><strong>\"Mr. Bones\"</strong> — a robed figure with a deer's skull and a ram's horns, standing before a floating mountain, met in the dreams and visions of Galeth, Rhaegar, Manfred, Jeffrey, and others. He speaks of a trillion souls, and seems tied to a triad of dead-world relics: the Black Altar, the cube, and the pyramid (the Heart of the Mountain).</p>\n\n<h3>Manfred's identities (you)</h3>\n<p><strong>Manfred Highrock / Endrun Ukinri</strong> — orphan of the Tidewash, former Prince of Swords of the Brotherhood of Swords, now Dark Knight of Lorenthar under Rhaegar. He wields rune magic (blood needles) and Sankari blades, and quietly builds toward Dark Knights of his own.</p>"};

const esc = (obj) => JSON.stringify(obj).replace(/'/g, "''");

